<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Ceibal</h1><p class="st-meta">400 CE – 930 CE (approx.) · capital Ceibal</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Maya city on the Pasión river with one of the oldest ceremonial plazas in the lowlands, which unusually flourished during the Terminal Classic collapse.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ceibal (Seibal) was founded about 1000 BCE, when early villagers built a ceremonial plaza, making it among the earliest Maya centres. After a Preclassic peak and a period of decline, a royal dynasty is recorded in the Classic period from around 400 CE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 735 Ceibal was conquered by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dos-pilas">Dos Pilas</a>, whose king captured its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yichaak-bahlam">Yich'aak B'ahlam</a>, and it became a subordinate centre. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petexbatun">Petexbatun</a> kingdom, Ceibal revived under the lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/watul-katel">Wat'ul K'atel</a>, installed in 829, whose stelae show foreign-looking dress and new styles, perhaps linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-coast-of-mexico">Gulf Coast</a> or northern contacts.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ceibal was one of the last southern lowland cities to raise monuments, until about 889, and was abandoned by about 930. Its long sequence makes it important for studying both the origins and the collapse of Maya civilisation.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 400 CE – 930 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:67.66%;width:10.55%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 CE – 930 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-ce